# TT.ESCALATION_INTERFACE -- escalate_os and escalate_customer called STATICALLY (a PL/SQL / PL/pgSQL
# caller with its own exception handler, like MC.MONITORING_TICKET_PROCESSES would be) for an
# event on a CLOSED ticket (status 102). S-ESC-009: both routines first UPDATE the ticket
# (escalation_status_id := 10, last_escalation_date := now) and then call addevent_new, which
# re-raises its ticket_closed error (Oracle -20980 -> canonical OS980) -- no handler in either
# routine, so the error reaches the caller. Both engines agree on the error code.
#
# known.diff -- the transaction-scope delta migration/spec/tt.escalation_interface.md records
# under "Assumptions / Unresolved" for XCUT-05, now observed live: when the CALLER's handler
# catches ticket_closed, Oracle keeps the UPDATE that ran before the failing statement (the
# ticket ends in escalation status 10 with last_escalation_date set), whereas PL/pgSQL's
# EXCEPTION block is a subtransaction and rolls back everything the failed CALL did (the ticket
# stays at 0 / NULL). Not a translation defect and not a correction either: an engine semantics
# difference that the spec explicitly does not decide ("arguably the PostgreSQL outcome is the
# saner one"). Recorded here so the decision (XCUT-05) has live evidence; the dynamic-dispatch
# path (tt__event_interface__exec_actions step 09) does NOT show it, because Oracle rolls back
# the whole failing EXECUTE IMMEDIATE statement there.
#
# ORA_USER=system: fixtures insert into stop.company, mss.host, mss.service, mss.host2service.
ORA_USER=system
COMMITS=no
COMPARE_MSG=no
DESCRIPTION=escalate_os / escalate_customer on a closed ticket from a handling caller: ticket_closed (OS980) on both engines; Oracle keeps the pre-error UPDATE, PostgreSQL rolls it back (XCUT-05 transaction-scope delta)
